      "notes": [
        "**Good news: Hebrew tells the time the way English does.** אַרְבַּע וָחֵצִי is literally \"four and a half\" — that's **4:30**, just like \"four-thirty\" (or British \"half four\"). Hebrew counts **forward** from the hour that has passed. (Speakers of German or Dutch, where \"halb fünf\" means 4:30, need to watch out here; English speakers don't.)",
        "**Remember it in one sentence: Hebrew names the hour that has ALREADY passed, then adds.** שֶׁבַע וָחֵצִי = 7:30. תֵּשַׁע וְרֶבַע = 9:15. When in doubt, convert to digits first.",
        "**Times use the FEMININE numbers** — שָׁעָה (hour) is a feminine word. So בְּאַרְבַּע (not בְּאַרְבָּעָה), בְּשָׁלוֹשׁ, בְּשֶׁבַע.",
        "**For times just before the hour, use רֶבַע לְ־** (\"a quarter to\"): רֶבַע לְחָמֵשׁ = 4:45. Here Hebrew counts backward, exactly like English \"a quarter to five\" — but only for the last half hour.",
        "⚠️ **The days of the week are numbered, not named.** יוֹם רִאשׁוֹן (first day) = **Sunday**, יוֹם שֵׁנִי = Monday, and so on up to יוֹם שִׁשִּׁי = Friday. Only Saturday has its own name: שַׁבָּת. So \"the second day\" is **Monday**, not Tuesday — learners often slip by one day.",
        "**The Israeli work week starts on Sunday and ends on Thursday.** Friday is a half day, Saturday is fully off. When Israelis say \"the weekend\", they mean **Friday–Saturday**, not Saturday–Sunday.",
        "**בְּעוֹד** (*be'od*) = \"in ... (from now)\": בְּעוֹד חָמֵשׁ דַּקּוֹת (in five minutes), בְּעוֹד שָׁבוּעַ (in a week).",
        "**אַל תִּשְׁכַּח!** = \"don't forget!\". Negative commands in Hebrew use **אַל** + the future form, not לֹא. To a woman: אַל תִּשְׁכְּחִי."
      ],
